dent :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383304} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e22321c-1630-581b-a2cf-b182baccc9d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a3109e9-f31c-5d6f-a6b9-341111eed328" :question/math-academy-id "q-132580" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=e.$ Given that the point $P(e,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{x}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(e,2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(e,2).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(e,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(e,2) & =2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{e}=4-e\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e7ce596-2d30-587f-8420-45e62e13d5dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383309} {:answer/id #uuid "877b2fa4-194f-5fc4-8176-fe7bfbedcc56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\pi }{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383310} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca68eba-afe1-5148-a902-7b75f1cf86fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383311} {:answer/id #uuid "625a56ce-727a-5b05-a3f7-9bccf25f5e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383312} {:answer/id #uuid "2f162d22-7bab-5326-997a-02d3e40fadea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "877b2fa4-194f-5fc4-8176-fe7bfbedcc56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\pi }{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383310} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c8364fd-cb71-5621-b790-1b21e9be9448" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c4b5aa7-27d5-5875-960f-3c04a61ab9fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132582" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=\\pi .$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $1$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(x+y){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi .$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=1$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,\\pi ).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\pi ) & =(1+\\pi ){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =(1+\\pi ){e}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\pi }{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6357e5c2-cf46-5660-bc20-49dbba19cc28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385489} {:answer/id #